WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.460
GARBAGE SALARIES:
$8,888,684. That’s how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.440
the United States’ highest paid government&nbsp;
employee earns. Can you guess who this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:16.800
might be? Most people’s first guess is usually&nbsp;
government leaders. Maybe the president? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:23.880
the US president actually only earns $400,000&nbsp;
per year. Maybe supreme court justices? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:29.820
these guys only earn just over $200,000&nbsp;
on average. Ok, what about Jerome Powell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:36.480
he literally prints the money, so he should earn&nbsp;
a lot right? Well, even Jerome Powell only earns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.480
just over $200,000 per year. Now, don’t get me&nbsp;
wrong here, these aren’t terrible salaries by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.240
any means, but when you consider how much work&nbsp;
it takes to get these positions, it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.700
seem all that lucrative. If you’re a supreme&nbsp;
court justice for example, you’re literally 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.700 --> 00:01:00.180
in 9, and you don’t even earn as much as a junior&nbsp;
software engineer at Facebook. But, wait a minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.640
who’s the government employee who’s earning&nbsp;
$8.8 million then? Well, it’s not leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.340
or chairmen, or even politicians. It’s actually&nbsp;
the football coach at the University of Alabama:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:18.060
Nick Saben. In fact, it’s not just Nick who tops&nbsp;
the government salaries list either. The top 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.460
government salaries all go to football coaches&nbsp;
at various public universities. While this may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.260
surprising at first glance, when you consider how&nbsp;
much revenue a good football coach can bring in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:34.380
their compensations start to make sense. But, this&nbsp;
doesn’t explain why regular government salaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.320
are so garbage. For example, Jerome Powell is&nbsp;
literally responsible for printing trillions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:46.620
of dollars and inciting macro economic changes.&nbsp;
So, you would think that this job would be highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:53.100
compensated to attract the absolute best talent,&nbsp;
yet it’s not. But, you don’t even have to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.080
that high up the ladder to realize how underpaid&nbsp;
government employees are. Police officers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.600
for example, risk their lives to keep all of&nbsp;
us safe, yet they’re paid $70,000. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.400
teachers are responsible for educating&nbsp;
the next generation, yet they’re paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:15.360
$55,000. By far the worst one, however, is EMTs&nbsp;
who are directly responsible for saving millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.320
of lives, yet they’re only paid $36,000.&nbsp;
So, how did government salaries get so bad?

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.100
FUNDING:&nbsp;

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.500
One of the first explanations that pop up is&nbsp;
that government organizations are underfunded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.960
and this is why government employees are&nbsp;
underpaid. But, you don’t have to do much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.540
analysis to recognize that this is completely&nbsp;
false. Federal government spending currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:49.500
clocks in at just under $6 trillion, and the US&nbsp;
currently has a total of 1.87 million federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.800
employees. This means that the revenue per&nbsp;
employee clocks in at a whopping $3.2 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:02.400
per employee. For perspective, Apple stands at&nbsp;
$1.9 million, Facebook stands at $1.6 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.460
and Alphabet stands at $1.3 million. And these&nbsp;
companies are known as the highest paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.740
employers in the entire world. Apple’s numbers are&nbsp;
skewed due to their retail employees, but Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:19.620
and Google come in with median salaries of just&nbsp;
under $300,000. Conversely, the federal government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:25.800
pulls in twice as much revenue per employee as&nbsp;
these companies, yet the average salary is only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.440
$79,000. People often criticize private companies&nbsp;
for not paying their employees enough money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.920
but the average business actually pay out between&nbsp;
15 and 30% of their revenue as salaries. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:44.160
federal government, on the other hand, pays out&nbsp;
less than 2.5% of their revenue as salaries. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.560
it’s that bad. If the government matched&nbsp;
the average business and paid out 15 to 30%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:58.200
we would see average salaries between $480,000 and&nbsp;
$960,000. Even if they only paid out 10%, we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:04.980
see an average salary of $320,000, easily blowing&nbsp;
out the private industry. But, if that’s the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.980
where’s all the money going? Well, the answer&nbsp;
is that it’s getting stuck up across inefficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:16.020
government structures. In many aspects, the&nbsp;
government operates as more of a charity than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:22.500
a logical business. Take NASA for example. You&nbsp;
may have heard about their new SLS rocket. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:29.700
it’s definitely an impressive rocket, it's grossly&nbsp;
expensive coming in at $4.1 billion per launch and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:35.580
it’s not even reusable. One of the main reasons&nbsp;
for this is that NASA is leveraging Space Shuttle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:42.060
technology from the 1970s instead of the latest&nbsp;
and greatest. Why you ask? Well, it’s to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:47.760
all of the industries and jobs that were dependent&nbsp;
on the Space Shuttle program. While this is a nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:54.000
sentiment, it’s not really all that practical, and&nbsp;
this is why private companies have been putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
NASA to shame. The SpaceX Falcon 9, for example,&nbsp;
only costs $67 million per launch while the Falcon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.880
Heavy only costs $97 million per launch. Also,&nbsp;
that’s how much SpaceX is charging customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:12.060
so their internal costs are likely even lower.&nbsp;
And in terms of payload, the Falcon Heavy has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:19.020
LEO payload capacity of 64 metric tons while SLS&nbsp;
has a LEO payload capacity of 70 metric tons. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:25.920
SLS basically has 10% extra payload capacity&nbsp;
for 42 times the cost. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:31.200
the government doesn’t have a funding issue,&nbsp;
what they have is an ineffective spending issue.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.040
COMPLACENT EMPLOYEES:&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.720
We can’t just lay all of the blame on the&nbsp;
government though as much of the low salaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.040
are due to the employees themselves and what&nbsp;
they’re willing to put up with. It’s no secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.260
that government jobs are relatively chill, and&nbsp;
while you can argue all you want about how great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:56.280
a given company’s culture is, it’s simply not&nbsp;
comparable to the government. While the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:01.800
does have it’s own fair share of stressful&nbsp;
jobs, the vast majority are not. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.740
think about being a tax examiner at the IRS or&nbsp;
a clerk at the DMV. This laid back nature is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:13.140
one of the biggest selling factors of the public&nbsp;
industry. Not to mention, the job security. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.760
this also means that everyone who ends&nbsp;
up joining the public industry isn’t all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.320
that particular about the pay. They of&nbsp;
course want to be paid a livable wage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.320
but they rarely look for more. This means that&nbsp;
they rarely ask for wage increases or promotions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:34.620
and they’re definitely not job hopping to maximize&nbsp;
pay. This is in stark contrast to the private&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:40.800
industry which has very much trended in this&nbsp;
direction. Employee loyalty is at all time lows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.320
and the days of people spending 40 or 50 years at&nbsp;
the same company are long gone. The same, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:51.240
cannot be said about the government. It’s not&nbsp;
uncommon to see government employees who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.700
spent decades if not their entire careers at the&nbsp;
government. But, while the government has employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:01.680
loyalty, this simply leads to less competitive&nbsp;
salaries. When your employees are willing to stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.540
around regardless of whether you keep up with&nbsp;
market wages or inflation, it’s unlikely that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:12.180
actually keep up with market wages or inflation.&nbsp;
And given that most employees are complacent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:18.360
no one really does anything about. Now of course,&nbsp;
there are some unions who fight for better pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:23.760
and they definitely put forth a valiant effort.&nbsp;
But, at the end of the day, they're really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:30.300
fighting for peanuts. For example, this one union&nbsp;
is trying increase the 2023 pay raise from 4.6% to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:36.960
5.1%. I mean, they’re literally having to fight&nbsp;
over half a percent. In the private industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.000
the tone is more like: if you’re not willing to&nbsp;
give me a $20,000 promotion, I’m gonna to switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.460
to someone who is. Something else to note is that&nbsp;
a large number of government employees aren’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:52.320
there for the pay in the first place. Virtually&nbsp;
all government leaders are there for popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:59.100
power, and prestige, not for a paycheck. A similar&nbsp;
argument could be made for everyday employees as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:03.480
well. Many of them are simply there for the&nbsp;
health insurance and the retirement benefits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:08.820
These people usually have spouses who work in the&nbsp;
private industry or they themselves worked in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:13.860
private industry and saved up a bunch of money&nbsp;
before switching over. So, they don’t actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.960
have to worry all that much about saving for&nbsp;
retirement or maximizing their pay. All they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.780
to do is pay their dues and cash out the benefits,&nbsp;
and that’s exactly what they’re looking to do.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.640
NO CHOICE:
While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:34.200
much of the public sector can be explained by&nbsp;
complacency, this isn’t true for everyone working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.960
at the government. Unfortunately, a non negligible&nbsp;
portion of the public sector is filled with people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.980
who don’t exactly what to be there, but they have&nbsp;
no other choice. This primarily consists of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:49.800
that the private sector views as undesirable or&nbsp;
unhirable. This doesn’t necessarily mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:55.680
these employees are bad employees per se, but they&nbsp;
have traits that private companies just don’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:01.020
to deal with. For example, a given employee&nbsp;
may have a long job gap on their resume. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:05.640
this employee may have a perfectly legitimate&nbsp;
reason for this gap. Maybe they were taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:11.280
care of a sick relative or maybe they were sick&nbsp;
themselves, but private companies don’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:16.560
care about the reason. From their perspective,&nbsp;
this may be an indication that no one wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:22.860
hire this person. Also, it’s more than likely that&nbsp;
the person is rusty at the job. So, the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.860 --> 00:09:27.960
would rather just move onto the next applicant&nbsp;
than investigate further. Another popular hurdle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:32.640
for many applicants is a lack of networking.&nbsp;
You would think that truly qualified candidates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:38.160
would have no trouble finding a job, but this is&nbsp;
not actually the case especially during market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:44.100
downturns. In fact, it’s way more valuable to have&nbsp;
an extensive professional network than just simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.540
have the skills given that the largest portion&nbsp;
of hires come from employee referrals. From the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.920
company’s perspective, it makes way more sense&nbsp;
to bet on someone that a good employee is willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.900
to vouch for as opposed to someone that you have&nbsp;
to judge based on a piece of paper. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.460
the last major group of people who have a&nbsp;
difficult time finding private jobs is people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:07.620
with disabilities. While companies may be willing&nbsp;
to accommodate for someone with a wheel chair or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:13.320
autism, it’s unlikely that they’ll deal with&nbsp;
someone whos deaf or blind. Now, technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:18.840
it is illegal for employers to discriminate based&nbsp;
on an applicant’s disabilities, but if we’re being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.720
real here, that’s not exactly how things play out.&nbsp;
Also, employers are allowed to discriminate if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.580
they can argue that a given disability will limit&nbsp;
an individual from completing a job’s functions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:35.940
And this is not that hard of an argument to make&nbsp;
when the applicant is deaf or blind regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:40.620
the job. The government on the other hand, is&nbsp;
magnitudes more likely to give such candidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:46.200
a chance, and oftentimes, they’re the only&nbsp;
ones that give such candidates a chance. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.980
while these people may not want to settle&nbsp;
for lower compensation at the government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.980 --> 00:10:54.900
they really have no choice, and they&nbsp;
don’t have much negotiating power either.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:01.320
THE STATE OF GOVERNMENT JOBS:
Low government salaries are nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:06.780
new. The government has always paid less than the&nbsp;
private industry, but they usually made up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:12.000
this with better work life balance and benefits.&nbsp;
Over the past few decades though, the disparity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:17.220
between private and government jobs has simply&nbsp;
grown larger and larger. And given the record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:23.100
high inflation right now, the appeal of government&nbsp;
jobs is not nearly as clear. You can argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:29.040
the government offers greater job security during&nbsp;
the recession, but at what cost? We’re not talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.480
about a salary difference of $50,000 at the&nbsp;
government vs $70,000 in the private industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:38.728
We’re talking more like $50k at the government vs&nbsp;
$100k plus in the private industry. And that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.728 --> 00:11:42.000
even account for promotions. At the government,&nbsp;
each promotions gets you an extra few thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.200
dollars while a promotion in the private industry&nbsp;
usually gets you tens of thousands of dollars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:51.180
When you compound this reality over an entire&nbsp;
career, we’re talking about lower compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:55.980
on the scale of millions of dollars. So, if&nbsp;
you’re currently working a government job or are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:02.280
considering one, it may be time to reconsider your&nbsp;
options, but that’s just what I think. Would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:07.020
take a government job? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you think that government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:11.640
employees are underpaid. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.000
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
